[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; so I won\'t repeat all of that here. If you haven\'t watched that comparison yet, make sure to check it out. Instead, let\'s jump straight into how you can run MiniMax H3 locally, even with just [music] 8 GB of VRAM and 32 GB of system RAM. Now, that 32 GB of system RAM'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] is important. You need it for model offloading alongside your VRAM because the H3 [music] diffusion model is 21 GB. The NVFP4 text encoder is 15.7 GB, [music]'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the two VAE files take up around 5.8 GB combined. That immediately raises a serious concern. Will all this offloading cause heavy SSD swapping? In my testing, [music] ComfyUI\'s dynamic'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'memory management handled it far better than I expected. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; As you can see here, I didn\'t observe significant SSD activity from swapping during generation. This is the live generation of the 15-second clip you saw'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at [music] the beginning, and I\'ll include the full, sped-up generation process at the end of the video. Before launching ComfyUI, make sure you have a compatible Python version installed. I\'m using Python 3.12.'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Newer versions may cause compatibility issues, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; so keep that in mind. Next, make sure you have a CUDA version that properly supports your GPU. I\'m using an RTX 5060, [music] which is a Blackwell GPU'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with CUDA 13.2. You also need [music] the correct PyTorch build. Go to the PyTorch website, click Get Started, and select the appropriate options for your system. These are the options I used, but yours'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] may be different depending on your GPU, PyTorch build, and compute platform. Once you\'ve selected the correct options, copy the command generated by the website. Go to your ComfyUI installation directory, click the'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'address bar, [music] type cmd, and press enter. Paste the command into the command prompt window, and press [music] enter again. Next, make absolutely sure that ComfyUI is up to date because'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'support for MiniMax H3 was added only a few hours [music] ago. If you\'re using the portable version, open its installation directory and go inside the update folder. You\'ll find two update options here. I used this option because'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m running the ComfyUI nightly version. If you\'re using the stable version, use this updater instead. To be fully transparent, I haven\'t personally confirmed whether the current stable [music] build supports MiniMax H3.'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The update may take some time, so don\'t close this window until the entire process is complete. [music] Once the update finishes, launch ComfyUI and drag and drop the MiniMax H3 workflow into the ComfyUI window. I'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'build this workflow entirely [music] from scratch. It\'s a lightly modified version of the official workflow. If you want to try the original version, click templates. You should see three Mini Max workflows here, but only if ComfyUI has'

[04:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'been updated properly. This is the official Mini Max H3 text-to-video workflow, and this is my modified version. Here you\'ll find links to all the required models along with their correct storage locations.'

[04:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, this part is important. Under both the diffusion models and text encoders sections, I\'ve included two model options. You only need to download one model from each section.'

[04:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you have an Ada Lovelace GPU, such as an RTX 4060 or a Blackwell GPU, such as an RTX 5060, download the FP8 diffusion model for a speed boost. The Tensor Cores in these GPU'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generations can take advantage of FP8 operations. If you have an older GPU, [music] the INT8 model may be a better fit. For the text encoder, download the NVFP4 version only if you have a Blackwell'

[04:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'GPU, such as the RTX 5060. Otherwise, use the INT4 version. The video and audio VAE files go inside the VAE folder. Your chosen INT8 or FP8 diffusion model'

[05:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'goes inside the diffusion models folder. The Qwen 3 the text encoders folder. If you\'re unfamiliar with these locations, open your ComfyUI'

[05:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'installation directory, go inside the ComfyUI folder, and then open the models folder. These are the three folders where you need to place the downloaded files. Back inside the workflow,'

[05:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; these are the four loader nodes where you need to select your models. Make sure every model has been selected [music] correctly. Here is where you enter your prompt. Next to it, you have the sampling [music] and the decoding section. I use the same seed for all my'

[05:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generations and [music] set control after generate to fixed. You can change this setting if you want comfy UI to use different [music] seeds between generations. The default sampler included with the workflow [music] is'

[05:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'res multi step and I used it for every test. The scheduler is set to simple [music] and the step count is set to 20. This control sets the duration of the generated clip. Inside the resolution selector, [music] you can choose your'

[06:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aspect ratio and then set the target megapixel value. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Directly below it, you\'ll find a reference table showing the actual output resolution associated with each megapixel value. These are the generation times I recorded, [music]'

[06:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first using the INT8 model and later using the FP8 model once it became available. For my first full generation, I set the duration to 15 seconds [music] and selected 0.6 megapixels.'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'According to the size settings [music] reference table, this is the corresponding output resolution. I clicked run and within [music] a few seconds, I got the dreaded out of memory error that we 8 GB VRAM users are'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'constantly fighting. [music] I then reduced the target to 0.5 megapixels, giving me a resolution of approximately 540p and clicked [music] run again. Just like before, I received another out of'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'memory error. Finally, I selected the recommended 0.4 megapixel setting which produces a resolution of 864 [music] by 480 or 480p and started another generation. This time it worked. So, on'

[07:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'my 8 GB VRAM system, Minimax H3 can generate a full 15-second clip, but at that duration, I had to limit the resolution to 480p. For 10-second clips, I was able to'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generate at approximately 540p. [music] For 5-second clips, I could reach approximately 720p. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; I performed my initial tests with the INT8 model because the FP8 version wasn\'t available at the time. Once the'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'FP8 version was released, I loaded it, set the [music] weight type to E4M3FN, enabled Sage attention, and achieved significantly better generation times. Even with those improvements, [music]'

[07:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'MiniMax H3 still takes considerably longer to generate a clip than LTX 2.3. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; However, its consistency is remarkable. In my LTX 2.3 testing, I often needed multiple generations to get a strong'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'result, whereas H3 produced usable results far more consistently. You can also interpolate the generated frames with this workflow if you want smoother motion. However, I found that interpolation can remove that cinematic'

[08:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'feeling from the footage, so I prefer to use it only on selected clips. Finally, [music] you can use the RTX super resolution workflow to upscale your videos to 1080p, 1440p, 4K, or any other resolution you prefer.'

[08:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Just make sure the frame rate of your input video matches the frame rate inside the video combine node. Otherwise, the timing can fall out of sync. One clear limitation I noticed appears when generating wide shots'

[08:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] containing distant faces, especially at lower resolutions such as 540p. Facial details can quickly lose their structure [music] and become distorted. When I changed the prompt to use a medium close-up and kept the subject'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'much closer to the camera, the problem largely disappeared. You\'ll almost certainly encounter other issues when experimenting with different scenes, compositions, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and types of movement. Don\'t expect perfect results every time, especially'

[09:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'before you\'ve developed a better understanding of how the model responds to different prompts. [music] Let me know about your results in the comments below. Drop a like if you found this tutorial helpful, and subscribe to the channel for more tutorials like'

